{"slug":"maps","pages":[{"names":["deg2km","km2deg","deg2nm","nm2deg","deg2sm","sm2deg","km2rad","rad2km"],"summary":"Arcs of a great circle: degrees or radians of arc as lengths, and back.","body":["On a sphere of radius r, an arc of θ radians is r·θ long. These use the Earth's mean radius, 6371 km, unless a radius (in the length's unit) or a body's name (`'moon'`, `'mars'`, ...) is given: kilometers, nautical miles (one minute of arc is close to one nautical mile) and statute miles."],"syntax":[["km = deg2km(deg)","Degrees of arc as kilometers."],["km = deg2km(deg, radius)","On a sphere of another radius (or a body's name)."],["deg = km2deg(km)","Kilometers as degrees of arc."],["nm = deg2nm(deg)","As nautical miles (`nm2deg` back)."],["sm = deg2sm(deg)","As statute miles (`sm2deg` back)."],["rad = km2rad(km)","Kilometers as radians of arc (`rad2km` back)."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"One degree, and a minute of arc","intro":"","code":"deg2km(1)\ndeg2nm(1/60)\ndeg2km(1, 'moon')","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n  111.1949\n\nans =\n\n    1.0007\n\nans =\n\n   30.3234","figures":[]},{"title":"A distance in degrees as kilometers","intro":"","code":"d = distance(59.33, 18.07, 40.71, -74.01)\nkm = deg2km(d)","after":"","output":"d =\n\n   56.8411\n\nkm =\n\n   6.3204e+03","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["distance","unitsratio","km2nm"]},{"names":["km2nm","nm2km","km2sm","sm2km","nm2sm","sm2nm","unitsratio"],"summary":"Lengths from one unit to another, and the factor between any two units.","body":["A nautical mile is 1852 m and a statute mile 1609.344 m. `unitsratio(to, from)` gives the number of `to` units in one `from` unit for lengths (`meter`, `kilometer`, `centimeter`, `millimeter`, `nauticalmile`, `foot`, `surveyfoot`, `mile`, `yard`, `inch`) and angles (`degree`, `radian`)."],"syntax":[["nm = km2nm(km)","Kilometers as nautical miles (and so on for each pair)."],["r = unitsratio(to, from)","The factor from `from` units to `to` units."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Pairs, and any two units","intro":"","code":"[km2nm(100) km2sm(100) nm2sm(1)]\nunitsratio('foot', 'meter')\nunitsratio('degree', 'radian')","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n   53.9957   62.1371    1.1508\n\nans =\n\n    3.2808\n\nans =\n\n   57.2958","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["deg2km"]},{"names":["dms2degrees","degrees2dms","dm2degrees","degrees2dm"],"summary":"Degrees, minutes and seconds as decimal degrees, and back.","body":["A row `[d m s]` (or `[d m]`) is `d + m/60 + s/3600` degrees, the sign on the first part that is not zero; the other way, each angle becomes a row of parts, the sign again on the first part that is not zero, so -0.5 degrees is `[0 -30 0]`."],"syntax":[["deg = dms2degrees([d m s])","Rows of degrees, minutes and seconds as degrees."],["dms = degrees2dms(deg)","Degrees as rows of three."],["deg = dm2degrees([d m])","Degrees and minutes as degrees."],["dm = degrees2dm(deg)","Degrees as rows of two."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"A position written both ways","intro":"","code":"dms2degrees([59 19 46])\ndegrees2dms(18.0686)\ndegrees2dms(-0.5)\ndegrees2dm(59.3294)","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n   59.3294\n\nans =\n\n   18.0000    4.0000    6.9600\n\nans =\n\n     0   -30     0\n\nans =\n\n   59.0000   19.7640","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["wrapTo180"]},{"names":["wrapTo180","wrapTo360","wrapToPi","wrapTo2Pi"],"summary":"Angles brought into one turn: degrees into [-180, 180] or [0, 360], radians into [-π, π] or [0, 2π].","body":["Longitudes that have gone past the date line come back into range; a positive multiple of a full turn stays a full turn (360, or 2π) rather than becoming 0, and an odd multiple of a half turn keeps its sign."],"syntax":[["lon = wrapTo180(lon)","Into [-180, 180]."],["lon = wrapTo360(lon)","Into [0, 360]."],["a = wrapToPi(a)","Into [-π, π]."],["a = wrapTo2Pi(a)","Into [0, 2π]."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Longitudes past the date line","intro":"","code":"wrapTo180([170 190 360 -190 540])\nwrapTo360([-10 0 360 725])\nwrapToPi(3*pi/2)","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n   170  -170     0   170   180\n\nans =\n\n   350     0   360     5\n\nans =\n\n   -1.5708","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["mod"]},{"names":["referenceEllipsoid","wgs84Ellipsoid","referenceSphere","earthRadius"],"summary":"Reference ellipsoids and spheres: the shape of the Earth (or another body) for the geodesy and projection functions.","body":["An ellipsoid has a semimajor axis a and a flattening f, the semiminor axis b = a(1 - f); its properties include `Eccentricity`, `MeanRadius`, `SurfaceArea` and `Volume`, all in its `LengthUnit` (meters unless given, and settable). By name (`'wgs84'`, `'grs80'`, `'clarke66'`, `'bessel'`, `'international'`, ...) or EPSG code. `referenceSphere` gives a body's mean sphere, `earthRadius` the Earth's mean radius, 6371 km."],"syntax":[["E = referenceEllipsoid('wgs84')","By name, in meters."],["E = referenceEllipsoid('grs80', 'kilometer')","In another unit."],["E = referenceEllipsoid(7030)","By EPSG code."],["E = wgs84Ellipsoid(unit)","WGS 84."],["S = referenceSphere('moon', 'kilometer')","A body's sphere."],["r = earthRadius(unit)","The Earth's mean radius."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"WGS 84 and its numbers","intro":"","code":"E = wgs84Ellipsoid('kilometer');\nfprintf('a = %.3f km, b = %.4f km, 1/f = %.9f\\n', E.SemimajorAxis, E.SemiminorAxis, E.InverseFlattening)\nE.Eccentricity\nfprintf('mean radius %.4f km; earthRadius %.0f km\\n', E.MeanRadius, earthRadius('kilometer'))\nS = referenceSphere('moon', 'kilometer');\nS.Radius","after":"","output":"a = 6378.137 km, b = 6356.7523 km, 1/f = 298.257223563\nans =\n\n    0.0818\n\nmean radius 6371.0088 km; earthRadius 6371 km\nans =\n\n   1.7374e+03","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["distance","geodetic2ecef"]},{"names":["distance","azimuth"],"summary":"The distance and the azimuth between points: along a great circle, or along a rhumb line of constant heading.","body":["Without an ellipsoid the distance is in degrees of arc on a sphere (`deg2km` makes it kilometers); with one, the geodesic length on it, in its units. Azimuths are clockwise from north. `'rh'` measures along a rhumb line instead, which crosses every meridian at the same angle and is longer except along a meridian or the equator."],"syntax":[["d = distance(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2)","In degrees of arc."],["[d, az] = distance(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2, ellipsoid)","The geodesic on an ellipsoid, with the azimuth."],["d = distance('rh', lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2)","Along a rhumb line."],["az = azimuth(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2)","The azimuth at the first point."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Stockholm to New York","intro":"","code":"[d, az] = distance(59.33, 18.07, 40.71, -74.01);\nkm = deg2km(d), az\nkm_wgs84 = distance(59.33, 18.07, 40.71, -74.01, wgs84Ellipsoid('kilometer'))\nkm_rhumb = deg2km(distance('rh', 59.33, 18.07, 40.71, -74.01))","after":"","output":"km =\n\n   6.3204e+03\n\naz =\n\n  295.1938\n\nkm_wgs84 =\n\n   6.3378e+03\n\nkm_rhumb =\n\n   6.7890e+03","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["reckon","track2","deg2km"]},{"names":["reckon"],"summary":"The point at a distance and azimuth from another.","body":["The other half of `distance`: from a start, go a distance (degrees of arc on a sphere, or the ellipsoid's units) in a direction (clockwise from north), along a great circle, or with `'rh'` a rhumb line."],"syntax":[["[lat2, lon2] = reckon(lat, lon, arclen, az)","On a sphere."],["[lat2, lon2] = reckon(lat, lon, arclen, az, ellipsoid)","On an ellipsoid."],["[lat2, lon2] = reckon('rh', ...)","Along a rhumb line."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"1000 km west from Stockholm","intro":"","code":"[lat, lon] = reckon(59.33, 18.07, km2deg(1000), 270)\n[lat2, lon2] = reckon('rh', 59.33, 18.07, km2deg(1000), 270)","after":"","output":"lat =\n\n   58.1623\n\nlon =\n\n    0.8328\n\nlat2 =\n\n   59.3300\n\nlon2 =\n\n    0.4394","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["distance","track1"]},{"names":["track1","track2","gcwaypts"],"summary":"Points along a great circle or a rhumb line: from a point in a direction, between two points, or as waypoints for rhumb-line legs.","body":["`track2` gives points along the great circle (or the geodesic, with an ellipsoid, or `'rh'` a rhumb line) between two points, 100 unless `npts` says; `track1` from a point in a direction, as far as `arclen`. `gcwaypts` gives points along the great circle that rhumb-line legs between them follow closely, as a ship's route is planned."],"syntax":[["[lat, lon] = track2(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2)","Between two points."],["[lat, lon] = track2(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2, ellipsoid, units, npts)","On an ellipsoid, with a number of points."],["[lat, lon] = track2('rh', ...)","Along a rhumb line."],["[lat, lon] = track1(lat, lon, az, arclen)","From a point in a direction."],["[lat, lon] = gcwaypts(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2, nlegs)","Waypoints for `nlegs` legs."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"The great circle and the rhumb line","intro":"On a plain plot of longitude and latitude the great circle bulges toward the pole; it is the shorter way.","code":"[lat1, lon1] = track2(59.33, 18.07, 40.71, -74.01);\n[lat2, lon2] = track2('rh', 59.33, 18.07, 40.71, -74.01);\n[latw, lonw] = gcwaypts(59.33, 18.07, 40.71, -74.01, 5);\nplot(lon1, lat1, lon2, lat2, '--', lonw, latw, 'ko')\nlegend('great circle', 'rhumb line', 'waypoints', 'Location', 'south')\nxlabel('longitude'), ylabel('latitude'), grid on","after":"","output":"","figures":[{"id":1,"type":"figure","number":1,"name":"","numberTitle":true,"width":560.0,"height":420.0,"color":[0.94,0.94,0.94],"visible":true,"version":9,"windowStyle":"normal","axes":[{"id":2,"type":"axes","kind":"cartesian","geo":false,"position":[0.13,0.11,0.775,0.815],"xlim":[-80.0,20.0],"ylim":[40.0,65.0],"zlim":[0.0,1.0],"xlimMode":"auto","ylimMode":"auto","zlimMode":"auto","clim":[0.0,1.0],"tight":{"X":false,"Y":false,"Z":false},"xscale":"linear","yscale":"linear","zscale":"linear","xdir":"normal","ydir":"normal","zdir":"normal","xgrid":true,"ygrid":true,"zgrid":true,"xminorgrid":false,"yminorgrid":false,"zminorgrid":false,"box":true,"color":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"xcolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"ycolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"zcolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"gridColor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"gridAlpha":0.15,"gridLineStyle":"-","fontSize":10.0,"lineWidth":0.5,"tickDir":"in","view":[0.0,90.0],"camTarget":null,"camUp":null,"xkind":null,"ykind":null,"zkind":null,"xtick":null,"ytick":null,"ztick":null,"xticklabel":null,"yticklabel":null,"zticklabel":null,"xtickrot":0.0,"ytickrot":0.0,"xaxisLocation":"bottom","yaxisLocation":"left","dar":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"darMode":"auto","pbar":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"pbarMode":"auto","camZoom":1.0,"visible":true,"layer":"bottom","fontName":"Helvetica","fontWeight":"normal","tickLabelInterpreter":"tex","tickLength":[0.01,0.025],"xminortick":false,"yminortick":false,"zminortick":false,"minorGridAlpha":0.25,"minorGridLineStyle":":","titleSize":1.1,"labelSize":1.1,"title":{"id":3,"string":"","fontSize":10.0,"fontUnits":"points","fontWeight":"bold","fontAngle":"normal","color":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"rotation":0.0,"interpreter":"tex","visible":true,"halign":"left","valign":"middle","position":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"units":"data","background":"none","edge":"none","fontName":"Helvetica","margin":3.0,"lineWidth":0.5,"lineStyle":"-"},"subtitle":{"id":7,"string":"","fontSize":10.0,"fontUnits":"points","fontWeight":"normal","fontAngle":"normal","color":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"rotation":0.0,"interpreter":"tex","visible":true,"halign":"left","valign":"middle","position":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"units":"data","background":"none","edge":"none","fontName":"Helvetica","margin":3.0,"lineWidth":0.5,"lineStyle":"-"},"xlabel":{"id":4,"string":"longitude","fontSize":10.0,"fontUnits":"points","fontWeight":"normal","fontAngle":"normal","color":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"rotation":0.0,"interpreter":"tex","visible":true,"halign":"left","valign":"middle","position":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"units":"data","background":"none","edge":"none","fontName":"Helvetica","margin":3.0,"lineWidth":0.5,"lineStyle":"-"},"ylabel":{"id":5,"string":"latitude","fontSize":10.0,"fontUnits":"points","fontWeight":"normal","fontAngle":"normal","color":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"rotation":90.0,"interpreter":"tex","visible":true,"halign":"left","valign":"middle","position":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"units":"data","background":"none","edge":"none","fontName":"Helvetica","margin":3.0,"lineWidth":0.5,"lineStyle":"-"},"zlabel":{"id":6,"string":"","fontSize":10.0,"fontUnits":"points","fontWeight":"normal","fontAngle":"normal","color":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"rotation":90.0,"interpreter":"tex","visible":true,"halign":"left","valign":"middle","position":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"units":"data","background":"none","edge":"none","fontName":"Helvetica","margin":3.0,"lineWidth":0.5,"lineStyle":"-"},"cmap":{"$ref":"parula"},"children":[{"id":8,"type":"line","visible":true,"displayName":"great circle","clip":true,"bdf":false,"hit":true,"cmenu":null,"x":{"$":"","shape":[1,100],"dt":"f4"},"y":{"$":"","shape":[1,100],"dt":"f4"},"z":null,"color":[0.0,0.447,0.741],"lineStyle":"-","lineWidth":0.5,"marker":"none","markerSize":6.0,"mfc":"none","mec":"auto"},{"id":9,"type":"line","visible":true,"displayName":"rhumb line","clip":true,"bdf":false,"hit":true,"cmenu":null,"x":{"$":"","shape":[1,100],"dt":"f4"},"y":{"$":"","shape":[1,100],"dt":"f4"},"z":null,"color":[0.85,0.325,0.098],"lineStyle":"--","lineWidth":0.5,"marker":"none","markerSize":6.0,"mfc":"none","mec":"auto"},{"id":10,"type":"line","visible":true,"displayName":"waypoints","clip":true,"bdf":false,"hit":true,"cmenu":null,"x":{"$":"XI+QQW/VkcCkeOXBkRFDwtWgfcIfBZTC","shape":[1,6],"dt":"f4"},"y":{"$":"7FFtQoqFeUI0OnVC6wViQjkjRUIK1yJC","shape":[1,6],"dt":"f4"},"z":null,"color":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"lineStyle":"none","lineWidth":0.5,"marker":"o","markerSize":6.0,"mfc":"none","mec":"auto"}],"xtickformat":null,"ytickformat":null,"ztickformat":null,"ztickrot":0.0,"polar":null,"texts":[],"yy":null,"units":"normalized","fontUnits":"points","parent":null,"bdf":false,"hit":true,"cmenu":null}],"legends":[{"id":11,"axes":2,"location":"south","orientation":"vertical","box":true,"fontSize":9.0,"visible":true,"textColor":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"color":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"edgeColor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"numColumns":1,"title":"","entries":[{"id":8,"name":"great circle","type":"line","color":[0.0,0.447,0.741],"lineStyle":"-","lineWidth":0.5,"marker":"none","markerSize":6.0,"markerFaceColor":"none","markerEdgeColor":"auto"},{"id":9,"name":"rhumb line","type":"line","color":[0.85,0.325,0.098],"lineStyle":"--","lineWidth":0.5,"marker":"none","markerSize":6.0,"markerFaceColor":"none","markerEdgeColor":"auto"},{"id":10,"name":"waypoints","type":"line","color":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"lineStyle":"none","lineWidth":0.5,"marker":"o","markerSize":6.0,"markerFaceColor":"none","markerEdgeColor":"auto"}],"interpreter":"tex"}],"colorbars":[],"annotations":[],"sgtitle":null,"cmap":{"$ref":"parula"},"menuBar":"figure","toolBar":"auto","pointer":"arrow","resize":true,"dialog":false,"placed":false,"closeDefault":true,"flags":{"wbdf":false,"wbuf":false,"wbmf":false,"scroll":false,"kpf":false,"krf":false,"wkpf":false,"wkrf":false,"bdf":false,"size":false,"resize":false},"cmenu":null,"menus":[],"toolbars":[],"controls":[],"cmenus":[]}]}],"notes":[],"see":["distance","interpm","plotm"]},{"names":["meridianarc"],"summary":"The distance along a meridian between two latitudes on an ellipsoid.","body":["The latitudes are in radians; the result is in the ellipsoid's units. On an ellipsoid a degree of latitude is longer near the poles, where the surface is flatter."],"syntax":[["s = meridianarc(phi1, phi2, ellipsoid)","The length along the meridian."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"A degree of latitude at the equator and at the pole","intro":"","code":"E = wgs84Ellipsoid('kilometer');\n[meridianarc(0, deg2rad(1), E) meridianarc(deg2rad(89), deg2rad(90), E)]","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n  110.5744  111.6939","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["distance"]},{"names":["antipode"],"summary":"The point on the other side of the globe.","body":["The latitude changes sign and the longitude moves by 180 degrees."],"syntax":[["[lat2, lon2] = antipode(lat, lon)","The antipode."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Through the Earth from Stockholm","intro":"","code":"[lat, lon] = antipode(59.33, 18.07)\ndeg2km(distance(59.33, 18.07, lat, lon))","after":"","output":"lat =\n\n  -59.3300\n\nlon =\n\n -161.9300\n\nans =\n\n   2.0015e+04","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["distance"]},{"names":["areaint","areaquad"],"summary":"Areas on the sphere or ellipsoid: of polygons, and of quadrangles between parallels and meridians.","body":["Without an ellipsoid the area is a fraction of the whole surface; with one, in its units squared. `areaint` takes polygons whose edges are great circles (parts separated by NaN); `areaquad` the region between two parallels and two meridians."],"syntax":[["a = areaint(lat, lon)","A polygon's area as a fraction of the sphere."],["a = areaint(lat, lon, ellipsoid)","In the ellipsoid's units squared."],["a = areaquad(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2)","A quadrangle's area as a fraction."],["a = areaquad(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2, ellipsoid)","In its units squared."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"A hemisphere, an octant, and a quadrangle in square kilometers","intro":"","code":"areaquad(0, -180, 90, 180)\nareaint([0 0 90 0], [0 90 0 0])\nE = referenceSphere('earth', 'kilometer');\nareaquad(55, 10, 69, 25, E)","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n    0.5000\n\nans =\n\n    0.1250\n\nans =\n\n   1.2160e+06","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["polyarea"]},{"names":["interpm"],"summary":"More points along a line in latitude and longitude, none farther apart than a step.","body":["Where two points are farther apart than `maxdiff` degrees, points are put between them: evenly in latitude and longitude, or along the great circle with `'gc'`, so the line can be drawn as a smooth curve on a map."],"syntax":[["[lat, lon] = interpm(lat, lon, maxdiff)","Points in between, linearly."],["[lat, lon] = interpm(lat, lon, maxdiff, 'gc')","Along great circles."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"A long segment filled in","intro":"","code":"[lat, lon] = interpm([0 0], [0 10], 2.5)","after":"","output":"lat =\n\n     0\n     0\n     0\n     0\n     0\n\nlon =\n\n         0\n    2.5000\n    5.0000\n    7.5000\n   10.0000","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["track2"]},{"names":["geodetic2ecef","ecef2geodetic"],"summary":"Latitude, longitude and height as Earth-centred, Earth-fixed (ECEF) x, y and z, and back.","body":["ECEF has its origin at the Earth's centre, z toward the north pole and x toward longitude 0 on the equator. The ellipsoid (a `referenceEllipsoid`) comes first; its unit is the unit of heights and coordinates."],"syntax":[["[x, y, z] = geodetic2ecef(spheroid, lat, lon, h)","ECEF coordinates."],["[x, y, z] = geodetic2ecef(spheroid, lat, lon, h, 'radians')","With angles in radians."],["[lat, lon, h] = ecef2geodetic(spheroid, x, y, z)","Back."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"A point and back again","intro":"","code":"E = wgs84Ellipsoid;\n[x, y, z] = geodetic2ecef(E, 59.33, 18.07, 30)\n[lat, lon, h] = ecef2geodetic(E, x, y, z)","after":"","output":"x =\n\n   3.1007e+06\n\ny =\n\n   1.0117e+06\n\nz =\n\n   5.4628e+06\n\nlat =\n\n   59.3300\n\nlon =\n\n   18.0700\n\nh =\n\n    30","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["geodetic2enu","ecef2enu"]},{"names":["geodetic2enu","enu2geodetic","geodetic2ned","ned2geodetic","geodetic2aer","aer2geodetic"],"summary":"A point seen from a local origin: as east, north and up, as north, east and down, or as azimuth, elevation and slant range; and back.","body":["The local frames sit at an origin `(lat0, lon0, h0)` on the ellipsoid: ENU and NED are flat, tangent to the ellipsoid there, and AER gives the direction (azimuth clockwise from north, elevation above the horizon) and the straight-line distance. The ellipsoid comes last; lengths are in its unit."],"syntax":[["[xEast, yNorth, zUp] = geodetic2enu(lat, lon, h, lat0, lon0, h0, spheroid)","East, north and up."],["[lat, lon, h] = enu2geodetic(xEast, yNorth, zUp, lat0, lon0, h0, spheroid)","Back."],["[xNorth, yEast, zDown] = geodetic2ned(lat, lon, h, lat0, lon0, h0, spheroid)","North, east and down (`ned2geodetic` back)."],["[az, elev, slantRange] = geodetic2aer(lat, lon, h, lat0, lon0, h0, spheroid)","Azimuth, elevation and range (`aer2geodetic` back)."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"An aircraft seen from a tower","intro":"The tower at 59.65 N, 17.92 E, 40 m; the aircraft at 59.70 N, 18.00 E, 1500 m.","code":"E = wgs84Ellipsoid;\n[e, n, u] = geodetic2enu(59.70, 18.00, 1500, 59.65, 17.92, 40, E)\n[az, elev, r] = geodetic2aer(59.70, 18.00, 1500, 59.65, 17.92, 40, E)\n[lat, lon, h] = aer2geodetic(az, elev, r, 59.65, 17.92, 40, E)","after":"","output":"e =\n\n   4.5054e+03\n\nn =\n\n   5.5744e+03\n\nu =\n\n   1.4560e+03\n\naz =\n\n   38.9465\n\nelev =\n\n   11.4827\n\nr =\n\n   7.3138e+03\n\nlat =\n\n   59.7000\n\nlon =\n\n    18\n\nh =\n\n   1.5000e+03","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["ecef2enu","enu2aer","geodetic2ecef"]},{"names":["ecef2enu","enu2ecef","ecef2ned","ned2ecef","ecef2aer","aer2ecef"],"summary":"ECEF coordinates seen from a local origin, as east, north and up, north, east and down, or azimuth, elevation and range; and back.","body":["As the geodetic forms, with the point given in Earth-centred, Earth-fixed x, y and z."],"syntax":[["[xEast, yNorth, zUp] = ecef2enu(x, y, z, lat0, lon0, h0, spheroid)","East, north and up."],["[x, y, z] = enu2ecef(xEast, yNorth, zUp, lat0, lon0, h0, spheroid)","Back."],["[xNorth, yEast, zDown] = ecef2ned(x, y, z, lat0, lon0, h0, spheroid)","North, east and down (`ned2ecef` back)."],["[az, elev, slantRange] = ecef2aer(x, y, z, lat0, lon0, h0, spheroid)","Azimuth, elevation and range (`aer2ecef` back)."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"The same point by either road","intro":"","code":"E = wgs84Ellipsoid;\n[x, y, z] = geodetic2ecef(E, 59.70, 18.00, 1500);\n[e1, n1, u1] = ecef2enu(x, y, z, 59.65, 17.92, 40, E);\n[e2, n2, u2] = geodetic2enu(59.70, 18.00, 1500, 59.65, 17.92, 40, E);\n[e1 n1 u1] - [e2 n2 u2]","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n     0     0     0","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["geodetic2enu","enu2aer"]},{"names":["enu2aer","aer2enu","ned2aer","aer2ned"],"summary":"Local coordinates as azimuth, elevation and slant range, and back.","body":["Within one local frame: east, north and up (or north, east and down) as a direction and a distance. The azimuth is clockwise from north, the elevation above the horizontal."],"syntax":[["[az, elev, slantRange] = enu2aer(xEast, yNorth, zUp)","From east, north and up."],["[xEast, yNorth, zUp] = aer2enu(az, elev, slantRange)","Back."],["[az, elev, slantRange] = ned2aer(xNorth, yEast, zDown)","From north, east and down (`aer2ned` back)."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"North-east and up","intro":"","code":"[az, elev, r] = enu2aer(100, 100, 100)\n[e, n, u] = aer2enu(45, 30, 1000)","after":"","output":"az =\n\n    45\n\nelev =\n\n   35.2644\n\nr =\n\n  173.2051\n\ne =\n\n  612.3724\n\nn =\n\n  612.3724\n\nu =\n\n  500.0000","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["geodetic2aer"]},{"names":["inpolygon"],"summary":"Which points lie inside a polygon, or on its edge.","body":["`in` is true for the points inside or on the edge, `on` for those on the edge. A polygon of several parts has them separated by NaN; a part inside another is a hole."],"syntax":[["in = inpolygon(xq, yq, xv, yv)","Inside or on the edge."],["[in, on] = inpolygon(xq, yq, xv, yv)","With the points on the edge."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Random points in a star","intro":"","code":"rng default\nt = linspace(0, 2*pi, 11);\nr = repmat([1 0.4], 1, 6);\nxv = r(1:11).*sin(t);\nyv = r(1:11).*cos(t);\nx = 2*rand(300, 1) - 1;\ny = 2*rand(300, 1) - 1;\nin = inpolygon(x, y, xv, yv);\nplot(xv, yv, x(in), y(in), 'r.', x(~in), y(~in), 'b.')\naxis equal\nsum(in)","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n   103","figures":[{"id":1,"type":"figure","number":1,"name":"","numberTitle":true,"width":560.0,"height":420.0,"color":[0.94,0.94,0.94],"visible":true,"version":5,"windowStyle":"normal","axes":[{"id":2,"type":"axes","kind":"cartesian","geo":false,"position":[0.13,0.11,0.775,0.815],"xlim":[-1.0,1.0],"ylim":[-1.0,1.0],"zlim":[0.0,1.0],"xlimMode":"auto","ylimMode":"auto","zlimMode":"auto","clim":[0.0,1.0],"tight":{"X":false,"Y":false,"Z":false},"xscale":"linear","yscale":"linear","zscale":"linear","xdir":"normal","ydir":"normal","zdir":"normal","xgrid":false,"ygrid":false,"zgrid":false,"xminorgrid":false,"yminorgrid":false,"zminorgrid":false,"box":true,"color":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"xcolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"ycolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"zcolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"gridColor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"gridAlpha":0.15,"gridLineStyle":"-","fontSize":10.0,"lineWidth":0.5,"tickDir":"in","view":[0.0,90.0],"camTarget":null,"camUp":null,"xkind":null,"ykind":null,"zkind":null,"xtick":null,"ytick":null,"ztick":null,"xticklabel":null,"yticklabel":null,"zticklabel":null,"xtickrot":0.0,"ytickrot":0.0,"xaxisLocation":"bottom","yaxisLocation":"left","dar":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"darMode":"manual","pbar":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"pbarMode":"auto","camZoom":1.0,"visible":true,"layer":"bottom","fontName":"Helvetica","fontWeight":"normal","tickLabelInterpreter":"tex","tickLength":[0.01,0.025],"xminortick":false,"yminortick":false,"zminortick":false,"minorGridAlpha":0.25,"minorGridLineStyle":":","titleSize":1.1,"labelSize":1.1,"title":{"id":3,"string":"","fontSize":10.0,"fontUnits":"points","fontWeight":"bold","fontAngle":"normal","color":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"rotation":0.0,"interpreter":"tex","visible":true,"halign":"left","valign":"middle","position":[0.0,0.0,0.0],"units":"data","background":"none","edge":"none","fontName":"Helvetica","margin":3.0,"line